/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  THE GMP INTERIOR of the production building (DEV.md 4c C97, the solvent
  suite brought indoors in C100).  The plot plan above sees the building as
  ONE box, the `productionBuilding` item -- its separations, its road
  access, its ignition; `envelope productionBuilding;` makes that box the
  SHELL of the rooms below, so every room must lie inside it (x -70..-8 m,
  y -18..18 m, z 0..10 m), and the items the rooms list stand inside it.
  Grammar and checks: docs/ai/case-layout.md, "THE GMP INTERIOR"; record
  docs/design/a-gmp-interior-is-declared-and-checked.md.

  WHAT GRADE, AND WHY.  This is an ORAL SOLID DOSE (film-coated tablet)
  suite, not a sterile one, and EU GMP Annex 1 -- the sterile annex, whose
  A/B/C/D vocabulary the engine speaks -- does NOT apply to it; calling OSD
  rooms "grade D" invites a reviewer's objection (the design-basis dossier,
  docs/design/apalutamide-design-basis-dossier.md, section 2 and red-team item 10).  So EVERY room is CNC (controlled,
  not classified), the industry's word for an oral-solid-dose suite; a
  company may add its own ISO 14644-1 ISO 8 at-rest target (an estimate of
  common practice, not a requirement: dossier section 2).  What DOES apply,
  and what this building is argued from:
    * EU GMP Part I Chapter 3: 3.6 (cross-contamination prevented by design
      commensurate with risk), 3.12 (production areas effectively
      ventilated), 3.13 (a separate weighing room -- the dispensary), 3.14
      (provisions where dust is generated);
    * EU GMP Part I Chapter 5, 5.18-5.21 (a quality-risk-management process
      with a toxicological evaluation, the health-based exposure limit);
    * WHO TRS 1010 (2018) Annex 8, the HVAC guideline for NON-STERILE
      products: 4.4 (facilities positive to outside), 4.5 (the manufacturer
      decides the cleanliness level and states whether it is as-built,
      at-rest or operational), 7.5 (air from the corridor to the cubicle),
      7.7 (where dust is liberated, the corridor above the rooms and the
      rooms above atmospheric).
  The engine's cascade rule asks a step only between rooms of DIFFERENT
  grades (and not when the cleaner side is CNC), so with every room CNC it
  is the AIRLOCK rules (bubble above, sink below, cascade between) and the
  CONTAINMENT rules that check the pressures below; the corridor-to-room
  direction of WHO 7.5/7.7 is declared by the pressures themselves.

  THE CASCADE.  Every pressure is declared to outside: the dust rooms
  +15 Pa, the clean corridor +35 Pa, gowning and the cascade airlocks
  between their neighbours, the personnel bubbles +45 Pa, the material
  sinks +5/+10 Pa (they draw air IN from both sides and exhaust it), the
  contained suite's own corridor +25 Pa and its process rooms +15 Pa, so
  air moves INTO the rooms where powder is open and everything is positive
  to outside -- consistent with WHO TRS 1010 Annex 8, 7.7.  The 10 Pa step
  is the case's design value: WHO 7.4-7.6 asks for an individually assessed
  differential with no overlap of the operating ranges and gives no number
  (dossier section 2).  The technical floor is +5 Pa; the nitrogen loop's
  room, which opens only to outside, is held 5 Pa BELOW outside by its
  extract, so a leak from the loop is drawn to the extract and not into the
  building.

  CONTAINMENT.  The API is BOUGHT.  Its supplier's safety data sheet
  classifies it Repr. 1B (H360) and STOT RE 2 (H373, male reproductive
  organs) (MedKoo Biosciences SDS rev. 11 Jun 2020, dossier section 2), and
  NO occupational exposure limit or band is published: this case ASSUMES an
  internal band OEB 3-4 (roughly 1-100 ug/m3, an estimate, dossier section
  2) pending the supplier's figure.  Every room where the API is handled as
  an OPEN powder -- dispensing, the solution preparation (the powder is
  tipped into the acetone), the spray drying (the dispersion, 25 % API, is
  discharged), blending, roller compaction, milling and screening, final
  blending, compression -- is declared contained: CONFIRM THE BAND (README
  section 9, question 2).  A contained room is reached only through a
  bubble (people) or a sink (material, waste) airlock.  Coating and
  packaging handle tablets, not open powder, and are not contained --
  revisit that if the band is high.

  HAZARDOUS AREAS, ROOM BY ROOM.  Indoors the ROOM is classified, not the
  item: the solution room is zone 1 (the manway is opened to tip the powders
  into acetone), the spray-drying suite and the nitrogen loop's room zone 2
  (a closed, inerted loop: only flanges, seals and the powder discharge can
  release).  The one ignition room, the switchgear, is at the far (west)
  end of the technical floor, beyond the declared separation.  The suite's
  powder discharge is also a combustible DUST (a zone 22 question the
  engine's one-zone-per-room grammar does not carry): the group's to assess.

  THE FLOWS (one way each; nothing goes back through a room it left):
    * material in from the WAREHOUSE (south) to goods-in and the dispensary;
      the dispensed API and polymer go in closed drums along the clean
      corridor and through a sink to the SOLUTION room, the coating powders
      to the coating-preparation room;
    * the PRODUCT starts in the SPRAY-DRYING suite: the dispersion goes
      through a sink into the contained train (blending -> compaction ->
      milling -> final blending -> compression), out of the suite through a
      sink into the clean corridor, then coating -> packaging -> secondary
      packaging -> back SOUTH to the warehouse;
    * PEOPLE come in at the south lobby (street clothes), gown, and reach
      the work rooms from the clean corridor -- each contained room through
      a bubble airlock;
    * WASTE (press rejects, dust bags) leaves the contained suite by its
      north return corridor and a sink airlock to the waste room, never
      through a room the product crosses.
  The clean corridor carries people and closed containers of material both:
  declared `shared` below, with the reason.

  The voids (the strip north of the corridor's east end, the corner beside
  the solution room's airlocks) are service risers and duct space: the
  spray-drying suite's gas ducts reach the nitrogen loop's room through the
  wall they share.

  EVERY POSITION, SIZE AND PRESSURE HERE IS AN AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTION for
  the group to replace with its own design; the grade (CNC throughout) is
  argued above.  The ISPE Baseline Guide Volume 2 (Oral Solid Dosage Forms,
  3rd ed.) and the ISPE Good Practice Guide HVAC are where a real OSD layout
  is argued, cited by title only (not open access).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
